# Target C — exceptional-face closure corank and Hall expansion

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** proof-only research theorem; theorem authority unchanged.  
**Base:** `cb7f17077452cf7c445bbd64f099b8967a5b57fb`.  
**Dependencies:** canonical core-only ZR/ZF mixed infinitesimal rigidity and the full-force-visible C65 normalization; audited-working stopped-train decomposition §142; audited-working no-bounded-return theorem §132 only for the zero-column interpretation below.

This note extracts an exact finite invariant from the complete stopped-train system.
It does not use a response-conductance estimate, a KKT coefficient floor, or a carrier
constant.

Let `C_face` be the linear closure matrix obtained by assigning one infinitesimal
angular rate to every complete stopped train and imposing vector closure around every
bounded nonquadrilateral face. Then

\[
\boxed{
2f-\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}
=\dim\ker R_C^T
=:\sigma_C.
}
\tag{1}
\]

Thus the row corank of the exceptional-face closure system is **exactly** the pure
contact self-stress dimension. Since the full mixed active matrix has left nullity
`s_Z+1` and the normalized KKT stress has nonzero diameter part,

\[
\boxed{
0\le\sigma_C\le s_Z,
\qquad
\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}\ge2f-s_Z.
}
\tag{2}
\]

More locally, for every set `S` of bounded nonquadrilateral faces, if `N(S)` is the set
of complete trains having an endpoint occurrence on a face in `S`, then

\[
\boxed{
|N(S)|\ge2|S|-\sigma_C\ge2|S|-s_Z.
}
\tag{3}
\]

This is a Hall-type expansion theorem for the actual stopped-train endpoint network.
It converts the linear defect-face population from §§142--143 into a rigidity
obligation: except for at most `s_Z` global scalar dependencies, bounded defect faces
must supply two independent train-closure constraints each.

The result does not by itself close Type II because a bounded-degree direction network
can satisfy (3). The remaining closing step must use the geometric directions or the
outer/diameter bracing, not endpoint incidence alone.

---

## 1. Setup

Work only on the connected nonisolated positive-contact core

\[
G=(V,C),
\qquad v=|V|,
\qquad e=|C|.
\]

All contact-isolated source points remain in the full source. No coordinate of a
contact-isolated point is inserted into any rigidity matrix below.

Let `H` be the full actual diameter graph after the C65 lexicographic normalization,
with `h=|H|`. Canonical ZR/ZF gives

\[
\operatorname{rank}R_{C\cup H}=2v-3.
\tag{1.1}
\]

Write

\[
s_Z=e+h-(2v-2)\ge0.
\tag{1.2}
\]

Let `R` be the number of bounded strict-rhombus faces and `f` the number of bounded
nonquadrilateral faces. Audited-working §142 gives a decomposition of all contact
edges into `T` complete stopped trains and

\[
T=e-2R.
\tag{1.3}
\]

Euler for the connected plane contact core gives

\[
v-e+(R+f+1)=2,
\]

hence

\[
\boxed{e-v=R+f-1.}
\tag{1.4}
\]

The §142/core ledger also gives

\[
\boxed{T=h-s_Z+2f.}
\tag{1.5}
\]

---

## 2. One angular rate per complete train

Orient every contact edge arbitrarily. For an oriented unit contact edge `ij`, write

\[
r_{ij}=p_j-p_i.
\]

For an infinitesimal contact flex `u=(u_i)`, the contact equation is

\[
r_{ij}\cdot(u_j-u_i)=0.
\]

Therefore there is a unique scalar `omega_ij` such that

\[
u_j-u_i=\omega_{ij}Jr_{ij}.
\tag{2.1}
\]

Consider one strict unit rhombus with cyclic oriented side vectors

\[
r_1,r_2,-r_1,-r_2.
\]

Differentiating its vector closure gives

\[
(\omega_1-\omega_3)Jr_1
+(\omega_2-\omega_4)Jr_2=0.
\]

The two rhombus directions are linearly independent, so

\[
\omega_1=\omega_3,
\qquad
\omega_2=\omega_4.
\tag{2.2}
\]

Thus the angular rate propagates unchanged across opposite sides of every strict
rhombus. It is constant on every complete stopped train. Write that scalar as
`omega_tau`.

Conversely, assign a scalar `omega_tau` to every complete train. For an oriented
contact occurrence `e`, let `tau(e)` be its train and prescribe

\[
\dot r_e=\omega_{\tau(e)}Jr_e.
\tag{2.3}
\]

Every strict-rhombus face closes automatically by opposite-side cancellation. The only
remaining cycle-closure equations occur on bounded nonquadrilateral faces.

---

## 3. The exceptional-face closure matrix

Orient the boundary of every bounded nonquadrilateral face `F` cyclically. Define the
vector equation

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{e\in\partial F}
\omega_{\tau(e)}Jr_e=0.
}
\tag{3.1}
\]

Boundary occurrences, not merely abstract edges, are used. By the bounded-face
simplicity theorem the boundary is a simple unit polygon, but the occurrence convention
also makes (3.1) compatible with the global plane-graph cycle-space bookkeeping.

Collect the two scalar coordinates of (3.1) for all `f` bounded nonquadrilateral
faces. This gives

\[
C_{\rm face}:\mathbb R^T\longrightarrow\mathbb R^{2f}.
\tag{3.2}
\]

### Lemma 3.1 — contact flexes are exactly the face-closure kernel

Modulo global translations,

\[
\boxed{
\ker R_C/\mathrm{Trans}
\cong
\ker C_{\rm face}.
}
\tag{3.3}
\]

### Proof

Every contact infinitesimal flex gives train-constant angular rates by (2.2), and every
bounded face boundary is a graph cycle, so its edge-vector derivatives sum to zero.
This maps `ker R_C/Trans` into `ker C_face`.

Conversely, take `omega in ker C_face` and prescribe every contact-edge derivative by
(2.3). Strict-rhombus boundaries close automatically, and bounded
nonquadrilateral-face boundaries close by (3.1). For a connected plane graph, the
oriented bounded-face boundaries span the real cycle space, including when the graph
has outer-face bridges or articulation vertices. Hence the prescribed derivative sums
to zero around every graph cycle.

Fix a base vertex and integrate the edge derivatives along contact paths. Cycle
closure makes the resulting vertex velocity independent of the chosen path. Changing
the base velocity adds one common translation, and every contact equation is satisfied
by construction. This proves (3.3). `square`

The constant train-rate vector

\[
\mathbf1=(1,\ldots,1)
\tag{3.4}
\]

is in `ker C_face`; it represents global infinitesimal rotation modulo translation.

Let

\[
r:=\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}.
\]

Then (3.3) gives

\[
\dim\ker R_C=T-r+2,
\tag{3.5}
\]

where the extra two dimensions are translations.

---

## 4. Exact corank identity

Let

\[
\sigma_C:=\dim\ker R_C^T
\]

be the pure contact self-stress dimension. From (3.5),

\[
\operatorname{rank}R_C
=2v-(T-r+2).
\]

Therefore

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sigma_C
&=e-\operatorname{rank}R_C\\
&=e-2v+T-r+2.
\end{aligned}
\tag{4.1}
\]

Use `T=e-2R` and Euler (1.4):

\[
\begin{aligned}
e-2v+T+2
&=2e-2v-2R+2\\
&=2(e-v-R)+2\\
&=2(f-1)+2\\
&=2f.
\end{aligned}
\]

Substitution into (4.1) gives the exact identity

\[
\boxed{
\sigma_C=2f-r
=2f-\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}.
}
\tag{4.2}
\]

This proves (1). In particular, the two scalar closure equations per exceptional face
are globally independent exactly to the extent that the contact framework has no pure
contact self-stress.

---

## 5. Mixed rigidity bounds the closure corank by `s_Z`

By canonical mixed rigidity (1.1), the left nullity of the mixed active rigidity matrix
is

\[
\begin{aligned}
\dim\ker R_{C\cup H}^T
&=e+h-(2v-3)\\
&=s_Z+1.
\end{aligned}
\tag{5.1}
\]

Every pure contact self-stress `lambda in ker R_C^T` extends to a mixed self-stress
`(lambda,0)` by putting zero on all diameter rows. Thus the pure-contact self-stress
space is a subspace of the mixed left kernel.

C65 supplies a normalized KKT stress whose diameter part is nonzero, indeed strictly
positive on every actual diameter row. That mixed stress is not in the pure-contact
subspace. Consequently

\[
\sigma_C+1\le s_Z+1,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{0\le\sigma_C\le s_Z.}
\tag{5.2}
\]

Combining (4.2) and (5.2) yields

\[
\boxed{
2f-s_Z
\le
\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}
\le2f.
}
\tag{5.3}
\]

In particular, if `s_Z=0`, all `2f` scalar exceptional-face closure rows are linearly
independent.

No positivity floor for individual KKT coefficients is used; only the existence of one
mixed self-stress with nonzero diameter part is needed for the strict codimension.

---

## 6. Hall expansion for every defect-face set

Let `S` be any set of bounded nonquadrilateral faces. Let `C_S` be the submatrix of
`C_face` consisting of the `2|S|` scalar rows belonging to those faces.

Any row dependence of `C_S`, extended by zero coefficients on all other face rows, is
a row dependence of `C_face`. Hence

\[
2|S|-\operatorname{rank}C_S
\le
2f-r
=\sigma_C.
\tag{6.1}
\]

Therefore

\[
\operatorname{rank}C_S
\ge2|S|-\sigma_C.
\tag{6.2}
\]

Let `N(S)` be the set of complete trains having at least one terminal contact-edge
occurrence on a face in `S`. The rows of `C_S` are supported only on the columns
`N(S)`, so

\[
\operatorname{rank}C_S\le|N(S)|.
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
|N(S)|
\ge2|S|-\sigma_C
\ge2|S|-s_Z.
}
\tag{6.3}
\]

This is finite and applies to every subfamily, not only to all exceptional faces.

For `s_Z=0`, every `m` bounded defect faces collectively touch at least `2m` distinct
complete trains. More generally all Hall deficits, over all subsets simultaneously,
are paid from the single global budget `sigma_C<=s_Z`.

---

## 7. Outer-to-outer train capacity

Let `L_{OO}` be the number of complete trains whose two terminal occurrences lie on
the outer face. Such a train contributes a zero column to `C_face`.

Conversely, audited-working §132 excludes a nontrivial train returning to one bounded
face, and the endpoint-multigraph theorem excludes a bounded singleton loop. Hence a
train with a bounded endpoint contributes a nonzero endpoint coefficient to at least
one bounded-face row. Therefore the zero columns of `C_face` are exactly the
outer-to-outer trains.

Since deleting `L_{OO}` zero columns leaves at most `T-L_{OO}` columns carrying the
rank,

\[
r\le T-L_{OO}.
\]

Using (4.2) and the exact train ledger (1.5),

\[
\begin{aligned}
L_{OO}
&\le T-r\\
&=h-s_Z+2f-(2f-\sigma_C)\\
&=h-s_Z+\sigma_C\\
&\le h.
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{L_{OO}\le h.}
\tag{7.1}
\]

There is a one-unit strengthening whenever `f>0`. The coordinate subspace supported
only on the `L_{OO}` outer-to-outer trains lies in `ker C_face`. If at least one bounded
face exists, this subspace does not contain the all-trains rotation vector `1`.
Therefore it gives `L_{OO}` independent non-Euclidean contact flexes. Mixed rigidity
maps them injectively to the `h` diameter first-variation coordinates. KKT stationarity
puts the image inside the codimension-one hyperplane perpendicular to the positive
diameter multiplier vector `beta`. Hence

\[
\boxed{
f>0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad L_{OO}\le h-1.
}
\tag{7.2}
\]

When `f=0`, the common rotation lies in the outer-train coordinate subspace and the
correct bound is only `L_{OO}\le h`; this is consistent with the exact pure-tooth
train-shear normal form.

---

## 8. Transposed direction-network interpretation

The transpose of `C_face` is a grounded direction network on the endpoint multigraph.
Associate a vector `y_F in R^2` to every bounded defect face and ground the outer face
at zero. A left-row dependence satisfies one scalar equation per complete train.

If train `tau` has bounded endpoints on faces `F,G`, its equation has the form

\[
(\varepsilon_F y_F+\varepsilon_G y_G)\cdot Ju_\tau=0,
\tag{8.1}
\]

with incidence signs `varepsilon_F,epsilon_G in {+-1}` determined by the chosen face
boundary orientations and the chosen projective train orientation. If the other train
endpoint is outer, the equation is

\[
 y_F\cdot Ju_\tau=0
\tag{8.2}
\]

after absorbing the nonzero incidence sign. The precise signs are irrelevant to the
rank and Hall conclusions above.

Equation (4.2) says the motion dimension of this grounded direction network is exactly
`σ_C`, not an uncontrolled face-topology error. This gives a concrete next target:
combine the phase-pure corridor directions of §133 with the Hall expansion (6.3) to
force more than `s_Z` independent direction-network motions or an outer-train capacity
violation.

---

## 9. Hostile audit and scope

The following failure modes were checked explicitly.

- **Contact-isolated source points:** retained in the full source and absent from the
  core rigidity matrices; no full-source affine-kernel extension is made.
- **Bridges/articulations:** allowed on the outer face. Bounded face boundaries span the
  cycle space even in their presence, and the integration in Lemma 3.1 remains valid.
- **Face occurrence signs:** retained in (3.1). The Hall proof uses only column support,
  not a guessed unsigned incidence matrix.
- **Pure contact self-stress:** not declared impossible. Contact minimality only makes
  every contact strong in the union of KKT supports; boundary KKT stresses may still
  have zero coefficients. The exact theorem therefore keeps `σ_C` and bounds it by
  `s_Z` instead of setting it to zero.
- **Same-face train returns:** used only to identify the zero columns in §7, not in the
  core corank theorem (4.2)--(6.3).
- **Theorem authority:** unchanged. The result is proof-only and depends on the
  audited-working stopped-train decomposition.

**Disposition:** exact proof-only structural theorem. It does not close C-II. The new
full-core obligation is to violate (6.3) or (7.2) using the actual phase/corridor
geometry, or to convert the surviving high-expansion direction network into a strict
diameter descent.